=== Affine $n$-Space and Algebraic Sets <section1.1>

#definition[
  Let $k$ be a field. We define the _Affine Space_ of _dimension
  $n$_ (or _Affine $n$-Space_) over $k$ to be the vector space:
  $ AA^n (k)=k^n=underbrace(k times k ... times k, n"-times") $
  We call the elements of $AA^n (k)$ _affine points_ (or simply
  _points_). We call $AA^1 (k)$ the _affine line_ over $k$, and
  $AA^2 (k)$ the _affine plane_ over $k$.
]<definition1.1.1>

#remark[
  When the field $k$ is understood, we may simply write $AA^n$.
]<remark1.1.2>

#definition[
  Let $k$ be a field, and $f(x_1, dots, x_n) in k[x_1, ..., x_n]$.
  We call an affine point $P in AA^n (k)$ a _root_ (or a _zero_) of
  $f(x_1, ..., x_n)$ if $f(P)=0$, where $f(P)=f(a_1, ..., a_n)$
  whenever $P=(a_1, ..., a_n)$. We define the _zero-locus_ (or
  _locus_) of $f(x_1, ..., x_n)$ to be:
  $ V(f)={P in AA^n (k) : f(P)=0} $<equation1.1.1>

  If $S cc k[x_1, ..., x_n]$ is a set of polynomials, then the
  _zero-locus_ of $S$ is defined to be:
  $ V(S)={P in AA^n (k) : f(P)=0 " for all " f in S} $<equation1.1.2>
  If $S={f_1, ..., f_r}$, then we write $V(S)=V(f_1, ..., f_r)$.
]<definition1.1.3>

#proposition[
  Let $k$ be a field, and let $S cc k[x_1, ..., x_n]$ be any set of
  polynomials over $k$. Then:
  $ V(S) = inter.big_(f in S) V(f) $<equation1.1.3>
  Moreover, if $S$ is finite, then $V(S)$ is a finite intersection.
]<proposition1.1.4>
#proof[
  By definition, ${ in V(S)$ implies that $f(P)=0$ for every $f(x_1,
  ..., x_n) in S$, so $P in V(S)$. Conversely taking $P in
  inter.big_(f in S) V(S)$, then $f(P)=0$ for every $f(x_1, ...,
  x_n) in S$. So $P in V(S)$.
]

#definition[
  Let $k$ be a field. We call a subset $X cc AA^n (k)$ an _affine
  algebraic set_ (or just an _algebraic set_) if $X=V(S)$ fo some $S cc
  k[x_1, ..., x_n]$.
]<definition1.1.5>

#proposition[
  Let $k$ be a field. Then the following statements about affine
  algebraic sets are true:
  + If $af cc k[x_1, ..., x_n]$ is an ideal generated by some
    set $S$ of polynomials over $k$, then $V(af)=V(S)$.

  + If ${af_alpha}$ is some non-empty collection of ideals in
    $k[x_1, ..., x_n]$, then:
    $
      V( union.big_(alpha) af_alpha ) =
      inter.big_(alpha) V(af_alpha)
    $<equation1.1.4>

  + If $af cc bf$, then $V(bf) cc V(af)$.

  + Let $f(x_1, ..., x_n), g(x_1, ..., x_n) in k[x_1, ..., x_n]$.
    Then $V(f g)=V(f) union V(g)$. Moreover, if $af$ and
    $bf$ are ideals in $k[x_1, ..., x_n]$, then:
    $ V(af bf) = V(af) union V(bf) $
    Moreover, finite unions of algebraic sets are algebraic.

  + $V(0)=AA^(n)(k)$, anad $V(1)=emptyset$. Moreover, $V(x_1-a_1,
    ..., x_n-a_n)={(a_1, ..., a_n)}$, for any $a_1, ..., a_n in k$.
]<proposition1.1.6>
#proof[
  + Let $af=(S)$, and let $P in V(af)$. Since $S cc
    af$, for any $f(x_1, ..., x_n) in S$, $f(P)=0$, so that $P
    in inter.big(V(f))=V(S)$, and $V(af) cc V(S)$. On the
    otherhand, let $f(x_1, ..., x_n) in af$, and $P in V(S)$.
    Bu supposition, there is a collection ${f_alpha} cc S$ such
    that:
    $
      f(x_1, ..., x_n) =
      sum_(alpha) g_(alpha)(x_1, ..., x_n)f_(alpha)(x_1, ..., x_n)
    $
    where $g_(alpha)(x_1, ..., x_n) in k[x_1, ..., x_n]$. Since
    $f_(alpha)(P)=0$ for all $alpha$, then:
    $
      f(P) = sum_(alpha) g_(alpha)(P)f_(alpha)(P) = 0
    $
    so $P in V(af)$, and $V(S) cc V(af)$.

  + Take $af = union.big_(alpha) af_alpha$ for some index $alpha$.
    Let $P in V(af)$, so for any $f in af$, $f(P)=0$. Now, $f in
    af_alpha$ for some $alpha$, hence $P in V(af_alpha)$ for all
    $alpha$. Conversely, if $P in inter.big_(alpha) V(af_alpha)$,
    then $P in V(af_alpha)$ for all $alpha$, so $P in V(af)$.

  + Let $af cc bf$ be ideals in $k[x_1, ..., x_n]$. Take $P in
    V(bf)$, then for every $f in bf$, $f(P)=0$, then for every $f in
    af$, $f(P)=0$, so $P in V(af)$.

  + Let $f(x_1, ..., x_n) in k[x_1, ..., x_n]$. Take $P in V(f g)$,
    then $f g(P)=f(P)g(P)=0$ so either $f(P)=0$ or $g(P)=0$, and
    hence $P in V(f) union V(g)$. Conversely, if $P in V(f) union
    V(g)$, then either $f(P)=0$ or $g(P)=0$, and so $f g(P)=0$,
    which puts $P in V(f g)$.

    Now, let $af, bf cc k[x_1, ..., x_n]$ be ideal, and let $P in
    V(af) union V(bf)$. Then for any $f in af$, or for any $g in bf$,
    either $f(P)=0$, or $g(P)=0$. This puts $P in V(af bf)$. Indeed,
    if ${af_(i)}_i^r$ is a family of ideals, and $af=product_(i=1)^r
    af_(i)$, it follows from the above that
    $ V(af) = union.big_(i=1)^r V(af_(i)) $

  + Observe first that $V(0) cc AA^(n)(k)$. Now, let $P in
    AA^(n)(k)$ have the coordinates $P=(a_1, ..., a_n)$. Then:
    $
      0(P) = sum 0 a_1^(d_1) ... 0 a_n^(d_n)=0
    $
    so that $P in V(0)$. Likewise, if $P in V(1)$, then:
    $0=1(P)=1+0(P)=1+0=1$, which cannot happen. So $V(1)=emptyset$.

    Lastly, let $P=(a_1, ..., x_n)$. Then $P in V(x_1-a_1, ...,
    x_n-a_n)$, since $a_i-a_i=0$ for any $x_i-a_i$. Now, let $P in
    V(x_1-a_1, ..., x_n-a_n)$. Then we have the system of equations:
    $
      x_1-a_1 &=  0 \
      x_2-a_2 &=  0 \
            &dots.v
      x_n-a_n &=  0 \
    $
    so that $x_1=a_1, ..., x_n=a_n$, which makes $P=(a_1, ...,
    a_n)$. Since $P$ was arbitrary, $V(x_1-a_1, ..., x_n-a_n)={P}$.
]

